From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Date: Thu, 29 Sep 1994 14:07:43 -0700 (PDT)
Subject: [#94-9-595] Eschatology, Resurrection & Game Theory

One bias in Tipler's _The Physics of Immortality_ is that it focuses
on the Judeo-Christian-Islamic monotheistic tradition, especially
Christianity, for metaphors and reasoning about eschatology 
(the study of end times), resurrection, and immortality in 
relation to Tipler's physical eschatology.  Tipler derives
computational capability and subjective time approaching infinity 
as his theorized closed universe collapses into a singularity, and
uses religious metaphors and reasoning (theology) to flesh out 
and describe his conclusions.

In part this bias is justified because Roman era Hebrews, Greek Platonists,
and especially early Christians spent a great deal of time reasoning
about omniscience (which Tipler equates to computational
capabilities approaching infinity), resurrection and immortality.  In 
part the bias is good marketing: most of his readers will be most familiar
with Christianity.  However, there may also be significant pagan 
tradition which Tipler has ignored.  If any of our formerly vocal 
pagans are still on the list, would they care to comment on pagan 
eschatologies in relation to modern global relativistic eschatologies 
(Tipler, Dyson, etc.)?  Also, it might be better to start with a 
"clean slate" than try to work in old eschatologies based on
Aristolean physics and hopeful superstition.  (Early Christian theologians
claimed to be fighting against supersition, of course.  My interpretation 
is that they tried to maintain immortality while minimizing 
superstitious axioms, but they couldn't get rid of superstition 
altogether, whence Christianity).

Another very interesting argument Tipler makes, which he elaborated on
in a discussion we had last night (Tipler gave a talk at a bookstore
here in Silicon Valley), is based on game theory and the assumption 
of computational progress.  That is, as the prisoner's dilemna
family of games is repeated towards infinity, with increasingly
perfect information, the agents will achieve _agape_, the pure
altruism and love that was championed by the early Christians
and attributed to God.  The motivation for the Omega Point to
resurrect the dead into a paradise (rather than a hell, or my Omega
Nielson Ratings scenario), is because according to Tipler
they will have achieved _agape_.

It's quite interesting that Tipler's resurrection depends on
applying game theory to theology, as well as physics and computer 
science to theology.  In some ways, the game theoretic arguments
are the most interesting, because they move us away from abstract
assumptions about computational capabilities, and provide a way
to start fleshing out the nature of such cizilizations/beings
without resorting to superstition.  Cf. Robin Hanson's economic analysis
of uploads in the current _Extropy_ (#13) for an economic 
analysis that reaches different, quite non-theological conclusions.  
Of course Robin is discussing uploads with computational capability 
larger than today, but still finite and nongrowing, rather
than Tipler's scenario of computational capabilities growing 
to infinity.

